# Intro
A single, fixed-mass star at the center of a uniform, periodic glass-IC gas
box, with photoelectric (PE) heating of the gas by the star's interstellar
radiation field (ISRF injection, no propagation) and real cooling. The
example compares a `GEARFeedback:with_interstellar_radiation_field: 1` run
against an otherwise identical `0` run. Gas near the star should reach a
higher equilibrium temperature with heating on, and the measured heating
rate should match the formula of the chosen heating model (Wolfire et al.
1995, Eq. 1, for the default). `isrf_photoelectric_heating_check.py` does
both checks.

## Propagation
`ISRF_propagation: 0`: only gas within the star's own SPH kernel is
illuminated. The example is about the thermal response to the field a
particle receives directly from the star, not about the transport of the field.

## Cooling
`disable_cooling_for_debugging` is `0`: cooling and heating are both active.
The ISRF also switches on the grain recombination cooling of the dust. It is
part of the heating-on run, so it lowers the net heating of a particle.

## Metallicity
Solar (`Z/Zsun=1`, `initial_metallicity` in `run.sh`): the dust heating and
extinction scale with the metallicity.

# Cooling library and heating model
`pe_model=<name> ./run.sh` selects the heating model. `run.sh` reads the
cooling library from the binary.

| Build | Heating model |
|-------|---------------|
| `grackle_0` | Not supported: `run.sh` stops. The Grackle grain recombination term returns NaN for gas without free electrons, and this mode estimates none for cold gas. Use `grackle_1` or higher, or geajay. |
| `grackle_1` and above | `constant` (default, Wolfire et al. 1995 Eq. 1 with efficiency 0.05), `wolfire1995` (efficiency from the grain charge, needs free electrons), `density_dependent` (only on a Grackle build that provides it; SWIFT stops at start-up otherwise) |
| `geajay_N` | `none`, `fixed_rate`, `constant_efficiency` (default), `grain_charge_epsilon`, `density_epsilon`; `run.sh` then reads `params_geajay.yml` and sets the network N from the build (tested with `geajay_3`) |

`wolfire1995` and `grain_charge_epsilon` heat through the free electron
fraction. The initial gas is neutral, so their heating is negligible here and
the sign check does not pass. Give the gas free electrons with
`extra_args="-P GrackleCooling:initial_nHII_to_nH_ratio:1e-3"` (Grackle) or
`extra_args="-P GeajayCooling:initial_hii_to_h_ratio:1e-3"` (geajay).

# Configure
```
./configure --with-chemistry=GEAR_10 --with-feedback=GEAR --with-cooling=grackle_1 --with-stars=GEAR --with-sink=GEAR --with-star-formation=GEAR --with-tracers=GEAR --with-kernel=wendland-C2 --with-grackle=path/to/grackle
make -j
```
For geajay, replace `--with-cooling=grackle_1 --with-grackle=...` with
`--with-cooling=geajay_3 --with-geajay=path/to/geajay`. The GEAR feedback
module registers the `PESpecificEnergies`/`LWSpecificEnergies` snapshot
output.

The radiation table is the stellar evolution table with the `Data/Radiation`
group. The pychem table generator produces it from its parameter file
`params/chimieparam_radiation_parsec_spectral_popII.py`; `run.sh` fetches the
published one.

# Run
Two runs are needed for the sign/magnitude check, identical except for
`with_interstellar_radiation_field`:
```
with_pe_heating=1 run_name=heating_on  ./run.sh
with_pe_heating=0 run_name=heating_off ./run.sh
```
Override any other parameter via the matching env var: gas density
(`gas_density`, atom/cm^3), gas particle mass (`gas_mass`, Msun), star mass
and type (`star_mass`, `star_type`), resolution level (`level`, N =
`(2**level)**3` gas particles), run duration and stepping (`time_end`,
`dt_max`, `delta_time`), metallicity (`initial_metallicity`), heating model
(`pe_model`), further swift arguments (`extra_args`) and thread count
(`n_threads`); see `run.sh` for the full list and defaults.
`run_name=<dir> ./run.sh` moves the run's outputs into `<dir>` once done, to
keep the runs' outputs side by side.

The quantitative rate check needs a SEPARATE, much finer-cadence pair (see
`isrf_photoelectric_heating_check.py`'s docstring for why `ISRF_propagation:
0` requires this): `delta_time`/`dt_max` orders of magnitude below the
sign/magnitude pair's, and a correspondingly short `time_end`.
```
list="-P Snapshots:output_list_on:1 -P Snapshots:output_list:rate_output_list.txt"
with_pe_heating=1 run_name=rate_on  time_end=4e-9 dt_max=6e-10 extra_args="$list" ./run.sh
with_pe_heating=0 run_name=rate_off time_end=4e-9 dt_max=6e-10 extra_args="$list" ./run.sh
```
`rate_output_list.txt` opens the measuring window one step after the field
first reaches the gas, so that the two runs start the window with the same
energy. Its times hold for these `time_end` and `dt_max` values only.
`fixed_rate` heats without the field, from the second step, so it uses
`rate_output_list_fixed_rate.txt`, which opens the window one step after
that heating starts.
The ISRF also adds a small dust cooling, so the reference run should carry
the ISRF too. With geajay, run the reference as `with_pe_heating=1
pe_model=none`, so that both runs carry the same ISRF, and the difference is
the heating alone. With Grackle, use `with_pe_heating=1 pe_model=wolfire1995`
on the neutral gas (its heating is negligible) and pass
`--reference-field on` to the check. Run the heating run with the model to
check, for example `pe_model=constant_efficiency`.

# Checking
```
python3 isrf_photoelectric_heating_check.py \
    --on-snapshot  "heating_on/snap/snapshot_*.hdf5" \
    --off-snapshot "heating_off/snap/snapshot_*.hdf5" \
    --rate-on-snapshot  "rate_on/snap/snapshot_*.hdf5" \
    --rate-off-snapshot "rate_off/snap/snapshot_*.hdf5" \
    --pe-model grackle-constant
```
`--pe-model` is one of `grackle-constant`, `grackle-wolfire1995`,
`geajay-fixed-rate`, `geajay-constant-efficiency`, `geajay-grain-charge`,
`geajay-density-epsilon`; each is compared with its own formula only.

Sign/magnitude check: bins gas by radius from the star at each run's final
snapshot, restricts the comparison to bins where the on-run's own ISRF
field is non-negligible, and checks there that (1) the heating-on
temperature is higher than the heating-off temperature and (2) it stays
below a physically-motivated ceiling (1e6 K) rather than showing signs of
a numerical blow-up. Produces a comparison plot (`--output`, default
`isrf_photoelectric_heating_check.png`).

Rate check: measures each illuminated, sub-2e4-K particle's own `du/dt`
over the fine-cadence pair's first inter-snapshot interval with a
non-negligible field, subtracts the reference run from the heating run to
isolate the photoelectric contribution, and compares the median with the
model formula. The bar is a bound set by the window; the script's
docstring gives it. The check fails if the heating is too small to resolve.
Skipped, not failed, if `--rate-on-snapshot`/`--rate-off-snapshot` are not
given.

The check judges with a bar derived from the mechanism: the response of the
cooling to the heating, plus a bound on the float32 rounding of the internal
energy (see the docstring). Give it the number of steps in the window,
`--steps-per-window N` (1 for the shipped output lists), and
`--cooling-response <Lambda' dt>`, the derivative of the net cooling rate with
respect to the internal energy times the window (dimensionless). The
script refuses a value outside [0, 0.5) and a missing or zero `N`. With `N`
given, the allowed lead of the heating run at the window start is 2/N unless
`--lead-tol` says otherwise. The script prints the bar from the earlier
definition (`0.5 P + 4 2^-24 / P`) and the derived bar, and judges with the
derived one.

For the Wolfire model on the shipped fixture (`pe_model=wolfire1995` with free
electrons, heating-off run as reference):
```
python3 isrf_photoelectric_heating_check.py \
    --on-snapshot  "rate_on/snap/snapshot_*.hdf5" \
    --off-snapshot "rate_off/snap/snapshot_*.hdf5" \
    --rate-on-snapshot  "rate_on/snap/snapshot_*.hdf5" \
    --rate-off-snapshot "rate_off/snap/snapshot_*.hdf5" \
    --pe-model grackle-wolfire1995 \
    --steps-per-window 1 --cooling-response 4.2436e-4
```
Values of `--cooling-response` for this fixture (`dt_max=6e-10`, the shipped
output lists, Grackle `official` build):

| Model | `--cooling-response` |
|-------|----------------------|
| `grackle-wolfire1995` | 4.2436e-4 |
| `grackle-constant` (reference run with ISRF on, `--reference-field on`) | 4.2762e-4 |
| `geajay-fixed-rate` | 4.2718e-4 |

They come from a one-zone call of the Grackle library, with the same switches
as the run, on the reference run's state at the window start: take the net
cooling rate at `u (1 +- h)` for each particle (`h = 1e-3`), form
`Lambda' = -(edot(1+h) - edot(1-h)) / (2 h u)`, multiply by the window length
and take the median over the illuminated particles. Another fixture, cadence
or library build needs its own values.

What the check can and cannot see. Scaling the whole reference is resolved at
0.3 per cent for `grackle-wolfire1995`, `grackle-constant` and the other geajay
models; for `geajay-fixed-rate`, only an error above about 4 per cent (its bar
is 4.3e-2, set by the rounding of the small heating per step). The response
lowers the measured rate, so an error of the reference that raises the heating
is partly hidden: on the Wolfire leg the check cannot see an error of about
-0.06 to +0.21 per cent of the heating, nor about 1 per cent in `G0`. The
margins of `grackle-constant` (6 per cent) and `geajay-density-epsilon`
(3 per cent) are thin: a 5 per cent error in `--cooling-response` would fail
them. The reference takes `n_H` as `HI + HII + H2I + H2II` and assumes
Grackle's default `local_dust_to_gas_ratio` (0.009387).
